{"findings":[{"blocking":true,"document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC9 (:161–172) defines final CI qualification but does not define which local checks and receipts are sufficient for reviews to converge. requirements/design.md:226–228 explicitly leaves that question for the spec; requirements/plan.md:24–25 supplies only 'focused and affected-suite receipts' without an observable selection rule. R9 (:223) could therefore pass with omitted affected-suite evidence. Define the required local evidence and the qualifying behavior when it is missing.","id":"F1"},{"blocking":true,"document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3 (:81–85) universally rejects findings missing the new fields, and AC4 (:93–96) rejects fixed dispositions without sweep records, while AC11 (:209) says earlier-contract runs keep working. The documents do not specify how legacy findings, dispositions and accepted evidence behave on resume or subsequent review. requirements/plan.md:130–132 versions only spec lint; R11 (:225) supplies no explicit legacy-contract compatibility case. Define the compatibility boundary and observable legacy-run outcomes.","id":"F2"},{"blocking":true,"document":"requirements/plan.md","evidence":"P9 (:158–162) suppresses a regenerable set described as 'node_modules, build/dist/out, caches, .gatereeve-current/' but does not define exact paths or matching rules for 'caches' and build/dist/out. AC10 permits non-interactive deletion when the resulting list is empty (requirements/spec.md:196), so this ambiguity determines whether ignored files are disclosed or silently deleted. Specify the default exclusion paths and repository-extension rules so R10's listing and deletion expectations are deterministic.","id":"F3"}],"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"The proposed delivery follows the authorized six-slice approach: requirements/plan.md:20–31 specifies slice PRs into tb-convergence-and-retirement and a ready, unmerged final PR into main. Hosted CI follows paired-review convergence. requirements/spec.md:104–106 preserves substantive repair validation, :139–141 preserves authority distinctions, and :174–181 preserves exact-head CI acceptance and existing denials. No release build, tag, publication or final merge is proposed.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"FAIL","summary":"Implementation approval is blocked by undefined local-review evidence requirements, legacy-contract compatibility behavior, and destructive sweep exclusions."}